(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-12-06"] = {"mth-12-06-m01":{"idea":"A line is a starting point plus any multiple of a direction.","steps":["Start from the point a.","Move along the direction b by any multiple λ.","So r = a + λb. Each λ gives a different point of the line."],"others":"a × λb and a · b are not points on a line. λa + b scales the starting point instead of the direction.","trap":"Point plus λ times direction.","answer":"r = a + λb, where λ is a scalar"},"mth-12-06-m02":{"idea":"Eliminate λ from the parametric form to get the symmetric form.","steps":["x = x₁ + λl, y = y₁ + λm, z = z₁ + λn.","So (x − x₁)/l = (y − y₁)/m = (z − z₁)/n = λ.","A zero in a denominator means the numerator is also zero."],"others":"x/l + y/m + z/n = 1 is a plane-like form. The form with + x₁ shifts the point the wrong way. The form with multiplication is not a ratio.","trap":"Subtract the point, divide by the direction.","answer":"(x − x₁)/l = (y − y₁)/m = (z − z₁)/n"},"mth-12-06-m03":{"idea":"The vector joining two points is along the line.","steps":["Subtract the coordinates of the first point from the second.","The direction ratios are x₂ − x₁, y₂ − y₁, z₂ − z₁.","Any multiple of these is also a set of direction ratios."],"others":"Sums of coordinates, squared differences and products are not along the line.","trap":"Subtract, do not add.","answer":"x₂ − x₁, y₂ − y₁, z₂ − z₁"},"mth-12-06-m04":{"idea":"Direction cosines are the direction ratios divided by the length.","steps":["Divide each ratio by √(l² + m² + n²).","That makes a unit vector.","Its components are the direction cosines (up to a sign for the sense)."],"others":"Dividing by l + m + n does not give a unit vector. The root of a plain sum is wrong. The squares l², m², n² do not give cosines.","trap":"Square, add, root.","answer":"l/√(l² + m² + n²), m/√(l² + m² + n²), n/√(l² + m² + n²)"},"mth-12-06-m05":{"idea":"The angle between two lines uses the dot product, with a modulus for the acute angle.","steps":["cos θ = (b₁ · b₂)/(|b₁||b₂|).","The angle between two lines is taken as the acute angle, so use the modulus.","cos θ = |b₁ · b₂|/(|b₁||b₂|)."],"others":"The cross product gives the sine. tan θ = |b₁ · b₂| has wrong units. sin θ = |b₁ · b₂|/(|b₁||b₂|) swaps sine and cosine.","trap":"Dot product gives cosine.","answer":"cosθ = |b₁ · b₂|/(|b₁||b₂|)"},"mth-12-06-m06":{"idea":"Skew lines are not parallel and do not meet.","steps":["In a plane two lines are parallel or intersecting.","In 3D there is a third case: skew lines.","They lie in different planes and never meet."],"others":"Lines in the same plane are coplanar. Intersecting lines meet. Parallel lines do not meet but are coplanar.","trap":"Skew means not coplanar.","answer":"are neither parallel nor intersecting"},"mth-12-06-m07":{"idea":"The shortest distance is along the common perpendicular, which is along b₁ × b₂.","steps":["The common perpendicular is along b₁ × b₂.","The vector between points on the lines is a₂ − a₁.","The distance is the component of a₂ − a₁ along b₁ × b₂: |(a₂ − a₁) · (b₁ × b₂)|/|b₁ × b₂|."],"others":"The other forms have the wrong numerator or denominator, like a cross product in the numerator.","trap":"Triple product over the cross product's length.","answer":"|(a₂ − a₁) · (b₁ × b₂)|/|b₁ × b₂|"},"mth-12-06-m08":{"idea":"Lines that meet have zero shortest distance.","steps":["The numerator (a₂ − a₁) · (b₁ × b₂) is a scalar triple product.","It is zero exactly when the three vectors are coplanar.","That is the condition for the lines to intersect, so the distance is zero."],"others":"A distance equal to the distance between the direction vectors is meaningless. Infinite distance is not possible. |b₁ × b₂| is not a distance.","trap":"Zero distance means they cross.","answer":"zero"},"mth-12-06-m09":{"idea":"For parallel lines, use the cross product to get the perpendicular distance.","steps":["(a₂ − a₁) × b has size |a₂ − a₁||b| sin θ.","Divide by |b| to get |a₂ − a₁| sin θ.","That is the perpendicular distance between the lines."],"others":"|a₂ − a₁||b| has no angle. The dot product would give the component along b. Without dividing by |b| the result has the wrong size.","trap":"Cross product over |b|.","answer":"|(a₂ − a₁) × b|/|b|"},"mth-12-06-m10":{"idea":"The coefficients of x, y and z in a plane equation give the normal.","steps":["The plane is r · n = d, with n = ai + bj + ck.","So the normal has direction ratios a, b, c.","Example: 2x − 3y + 6z = 7 has a normal along 2, −3, 6."],"others":"d is the constant, not a normal component. a², b², c² and 1/a, 1/b, 1/c do not give the normal.","trap":"Read off the coefficients.","answer":"a, b, c"},"mth-12-06-m11":{"idea":"Every point of the plane has the same component along the unit normal.","steps":["The unit normal is n̂.","The component of r along n̂ is r · n̂.","For a plane at distance d: r · n̂ = d."],"others":"r × n̂ is a vector. r = d n̂ is only one point. r + n̂ = d adds a vector to a number.","trap":"Dot with the unit normal.","answer":"r · n̂ = d"},"mth-12-06-m12":{"idea":"A vector from the given point to any point of the plane is perpendicular to the normal.","steps":["Let (x, y, z) be any point of the plane.","The vector (x − x₁, y − y₁, z − z₁) is perpendicular to the normal (a, b, c).","So a(x − x₁) + b(y − y₁) + c(z − z₁) = 0."],"others":"The ratio form is the equation of a line. ax₁ + by₁ + cz₁ = 0 is a plane through the origin. The + form has the wrong signs.","trap":"Dot product equals zero.","answer":"a(x − x₁) + b(y − y₁) + c(z − z₁) = 0"},"mth-12-06-m13":{"idea":"Find a normal from two vectors in the plane.","steps":["(b − a) × (c − a) is a normal to the plane.","The vector r − a lies in the plane, so it is perpendicular to this normal.","So (r − a) · [(b − a) × (c − a)] = 0."],"others":"r · (a × b × c) is not well defined. The form with a dot inside the cross is wrong. The + forms shift the points the wrong way.","trap":"Subtract a from each point first.","answer":"(r − a) · [(b − a) × (c − a)] = 0"},"mth-12-06-m14":{"idea":"The intercept form is read directly off the intercepts.","steps":["Setting y = z = 0 should give x = a.","x/a + y/b + z/c = 1 does that, and similarly for y and z.","So that is the intercept form."],"others":"Equal to 0 gives a plane through the origin. ax + by + cz = 1 has intercepts 1/a, 1/b, 1/c. a/x + b/y + c/z = 1 is not a plane.","trap":"Intercepts go in the denominators.","answer":"x/a + y/b + z/c = 1"},"mth-12-06-m15":{"idea":"Perpendicular planes have perpendicular normals.","steps":["The normals are (a₁, b₁, c₁) and (a₂, b₂, c₂).","Perpendicular normals have a zero dot product.","So a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ + c₁c₂ = 0."],"others":"Equal sums have no meaning here. Equal ratios mean the planes are parallel. A dot product of 1 is not the condition.","trap":"Parallel: ratios equal. Perpendicular: dot zero.","answer":"a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ + c₁c₂ = 0"},"mth-12-06-m16":{"idea":"Distance from a point to a plane is the plugged-in value divided by the length of the normal.","steps":["Put the point into ax + by + cz + d.","Divide by the length of the normal, √(a² + b² + c²).","Take the modulus to get the distance."],"others":"Dividing by a + b + c is not the length of the normal. Without any division the result is not a distance. Using √(a + b + c) leaves out the squares.","trap":"Squares under the root.","answer":"|ax₁ + by₁ + cz₁ + d|/√(a² + b² + c²)"},"mth-12-06-m17":{"idea":"Distance of the origin from a plane is |d| over the length of the normal.","steps":["Write the plane as 3x + 4y + 12z − 26 = 0.","Distance = 26/√(9 + 16 + 144) = 26/√169.","= 26/13 = 2."],"others":"13 is the length of the normal. 26 is the constant. 26/169 divides by the square of the length.","trap":"√169 = 13.","answer":"2"},"mth-12-06-m18":{"idea":"The angle between a line and a plane uses sine, because it is measured from the plane.","steps":["The angle between the line and the normal is 90° minus the angle between the line and the plane.","So cos of the first is sin of the second.","sin θ = |b · n|/(|b||n|)."],"others":"cos θ = ... would give the angle with the normal. tan θ = |b · n| has the wrong units. The cross product version gives the cosine.","trap":"Line and plane: sine.","answer":"sinθ = |b · n|/(|b||n|)"},"mth-12-06-m19":{"idea":"A line is parallel to a plane when it is perpendicular to the normal.","steps":["The normal is perpendicular to the plane.","A line parallel to the plane is perpendicular to the normal.","So b · n = 0."],"others":"b = n would mean perpendicular to the plane. b · n = |b||n| means parallel to the normal. b × n = 0 also means parallel to the normal.","trap":"Parallel to a plane: dot zero.","answer":"b · n = 0"},"mth-12-06-m20":{"idea":"Every point on the x-axis has y = 0 and z = 0.","steps":["Points on the x-axis look like (x, 0, 0).","So y = 0 and z = 0.","Its direction ratios are 1, 0, 0."],"others":"x = y = z is a diagonal line. x = 0 and z = 0 is the y-axis. x = 0 and y = 0 is the z-axis.","trap":"x-axis: y and z are zero.","answer":"y = 0 and z = 0"},"mth-12-06-m21":{"idea":"For parallel planes the distance depends only on the difference of the constants.","steps":["Take any point on the first plane.","Use the point-to-plane distance formula on the second plane.","The result is |d₁ − d₂|/√(a² + b² + c²)."],"others":"Dividing by a + b + c is wrong. Leaving out the denominator gives the wrong size. |d₁ + d₂| adds the constants.","trap":"Subtract the constants.","answer":"|d₁ − d₂|/√(a² + b² + c²)"},"mth-12-06-m22":{"idea":"A line perpendicular to a plane runs along its normal.","steps":["The normal is perpendicular to the plane.","A line along the normal is perpendicular to every line in the plane.","So its direction vector is parallel to the normal."],"others":"45° to the normal is not special. The position vector of a point is unrelated. Perpendicular to the normal means parallel to the plane.","trap":"Along the normal means perpendicular to the plane.","answer":"parallel to the normal of the plane"},"mth-12-06-m23":{"idea":"Two lines are coplanar when the scalar triple product is zero.","steps":["The triple product is (a₂ − a₁) · (b₁ × b₂).","As a determinant, it has rows (x₂ − x₁, y₂ − y₁, z₂ − z₁), (l₁, m₁, n₁) and (l₂, m₂, n₂).","If it is zero, the three vectors lie in one plane."],"others":"A sum of direction ratios means nothing here. Equal direction ratios means parallel lines only. A determinant of only the two direction rows does not include the point separation.","trap":"Include the vector between the two points.","answer":"the determinant with rows (x₂ − x₁, y₂ − y₁, z₂ − z₁), (l₁, m₁, n₁), (l₂, m₂, n₂) is zero"},"mth-12-06-m24":{"idea":"λ = 0 gives the starting point.","steps":["Put λ = 0 in x = x₁ + λl, y = y₁ + λm, z = z₁ + λn.","The coordinates reduce to (x₁, y₁, z₁).","Other values of λ give other points on the same line."],"others":"The origin and the foot of the perpendicular are not what λ = 0 gives. A point at infinity would need λ to be very large.","trap":"λ = 0 is the base point.","answer":"the given point (x₁, y₁, z₁)"},"mth-12-06-n01":{"idea":"Use cos θ = |b₁ · b₂|/(|b₁||b₂|).","steps":["b₁ · b₂ = 1·2 + 2·(−1) + 2·2 = 4.","|b₁| = |b₂| = 3.","cos θ = 4/9, so θ = 63.6°."],"trap":"Use the modulus for the acute angle.","answer":"63.6°"},"mth-12-06-n02":{"idea":"Use the point-to-plane distance formula.","steps":["Write the plane as 2x − 2y + z − 3 = 0.","Put in (1, 2, 3): 2(1) − 2(2) + 1(3) − 3 = −2.","Distance = |−2|/√(4 + 4 + 1) = 2/3 = 0.667."],"trap":"Divide by √9 = 3.","answer":"0.667"},"mth-12-06-n03":{"idea":"Use the shortest distance formula for skew lines.","steps":["b₁ × b₂ = (−2, 1, 3), with magnitude √14.","a₂ − a₁ = (1, 2, 2), and the dot product is −2 + 2 + 6 = 6.","Distance = 6/√14 = 1.604."],"trap":"Triple product over the cross product's length.","answer":"1.604"},"mth-12-06-n04":{"idea":"For parallel lines use the cross product of the separation with the direction.","steps":["a₂ − a₁ = (1, 0, 0) and b = (1, 2, 2).","(a₂ − a₁) × b = (0, −2, 2), with magnitude 2√2.","Distance = 2√2/|b| = 2√2/3 = 0.943."],"trap":"|b| = 3.","answer":"0.943"},"mth-12-06-n05":{"idea":"The angle between planes is the angle between their normals.","steps":["Normals: (1, 1, 1) and (1, −1, 1).","cos θ = |1 − 1 + 1|/(√3 × √3) = 1/3.","θ = 70.53°."],"trap":"Use the modulus for the acute angle.","answer":"70.53°"},"mth-12-06-n06":{"idea":"For a line and a plane use sine.","steps":["b = (1, 2, 2) with |b| = 3, and n = (1, 0, 0) with |n| = 1.","sin θ = |b · n|/(|b||n|) = 1/3.","θ = 19.47°."],"trap":"Sine, not cosine, for the angle with the plane.","answer":"19.47°"},"mth-12-06-n07":{"idea":"Put the point into the left side of the plane equation to find d.","steps":["The plane is 2x − y + 2z = d.","At (1, 2, 3): 2(1) − 1(2) + 2(3) = 2 − 2 + 6.","So the constant on the right side is d = 6."],"trap":"The plane passes through the point, so it satisfies the equation.","answer":"6"},"mth-12-06-n08":{"idea":"Find each intercept by setting the other two variables to zero.","steps":["y = z = 0: 2x = 12, so x = 6.","x = z = 0: 3y = 12, so y = 4.","x = y = 0: 6z = 12, so z = 2.","Sum = 6 + 4 + 2 = 12."],"trap":"Divide 12 by each coefficient.","answer":"12"},"mth-12-06-n09":{"idea":"Distance between parallel planes is |d₁ − d₂| over the length of the normal.","steps":["Write them with the same left side: constants 3 and 9.","|9 − 3| = 6.","Length of the normal √(4 + 4 + 1) = 3, so the distance is 6/3 = 2."],"trap":"Both planes need the same coefficients.","answer":"2"},"mth-12-06-n10":{"idea":"Distance of the origin: |d| over the length of the normal.","steps":["Plane: 3x + 4y + 12z = 26.","Length of the normal √(9 + 16 + 144) = √169 = 13.","Distance = 26/13 = 2."],"trap":"Same as the earlier MCQ.","answer":"2"}};
